  "input": "あなたは英語学習コンテンツの独立評価者です。以下の英語記事から「英語上級者が学ぶ価値のあるKey Phrase」を選ぶ課題があり、\n複数の匿名の選定結果(セット S1〜Sn、各5件以内)があります。各候補を下記rubricで採点してください。どのセットがどのモデルの結果かは不明です。\n\n【選定時に参加者へ与えられた条件(参考)】\n1. 本文中の連続した語句だけを抜き出す(一般化以外の変更・言い換え・追加・創作は禁止)。\n2. 一般化後5語以内。\n3. 固有名詞や特定の人物・物事を指す部分は、意味と文法を保って ~ / A / B / someone / something 等に置き換える(一般化できる場合は必ず行う)。\n4. 上級者でも学ぶ価値のある語彙・イディオム・慣用表現を優先する。\n5. 基本語だけで構成され字面どおりに意味が分かる表現や、上級者なら知っている易しい表現は除外する。\n6. 条件を満たす候補から5件選ぶ。適切な候補が不足する場合のみ5件未満とし、数合わせはしない。\n\n【採点rubric(各候補、各0〜2の整数)】\n- G 一般化の適切さ: 2=本文固有の固有名詞・特定の人物/物事が適切に一般化されている、または一般化すべき要素がなく原文のままで適切 / 1=一般化が不十分または過剰、あるいは軽微な不自然さ / 0=一般化すべき固有要素が残って学習価値を損なう、または一般化で意味・文法が壊れている。\n- V 上級者向け学習価値: 2=上級者でも学ぶ価値が明確(句動詞・イディオム・コロケーション・語法・語彙として) / 1=価値はあるが中程度、または一部平易 / 0=学習価値がほぼない。\n- E 易しさの排除: 2=易しい表現ではない(意味が字面から自明でない) / 1=やや易しいが慣用的な意味や語法の学びはある / 0=基本語のみで字面どおり、上級者なら当然知っている。\n- N 単位の自然さ: 2=自然で完結した学習単位 / 1=余分な語または不足があるが許容 / 0=中途半端に切られている・不自然。\n- needs_generalization: その候補の元の表現に、一般化(~, A/B, someone, something等)すべき要素が本文上あったか(true/false)。\nセット単位: padding = 5件を埋めるために学習価値の低い候補(V<=0またはE<=0)を入れていると判断するか(true/false)。\n\n【注意】\n- 本文との一致・語数は別途機械検証するため、ここでは採点対象外。ただし本文に明らかに存在しない表現と気付いた場合のみ noteに \"not_in_text\" と書く。\n- 候補の優劣を、セットの並び順やセットIDから推測しない。各候補を独立に採点する。\n- 候補が0件のセットは candidates を空配列にする。\n\n【出力】JSONのみ(説明文・コードフェンス不要)。noteは通常は空文字、本文に存在しない表現と気付いた場合のみ \"not_in_text\"。形式:\n{\"sets\":[{\"set_id\":\"S1\",\"padding\":false,\"candidates\":[{\"idx\":1,\"G\":2,\"V\":2,\"E\":1,\"N\":2,\"needs_generalization\":false,\"note\":\"\"}]}]}\n\n\n【記事】\n\n# If You Had to Watch a Stone for Five Minutes, Would Your “Bad-Guy Switch” Turn On?\n\nA stone. Still a stone. Surely something will happen soon—no, it’s a stone. Nothing happens on the screen. That goes on for five minutes. But you, the viewer, can still get one thing done: cut someone else’s reward.\n\nThis sounds grim all of a sudden, but it’s about an online experiment with participants in the United States. They were randomly assigned to watch either a five-minute video of a stone, where nothing happened, or a video of a magician.\n\nDuring that time, they had three choices: reduce another participant’s reward, increase it, or do nothing. They could change it by 10 cents at a time, up to a maximum of 50 cents. The stone on the screen stayed silent, while the viewers were handed “a little power.”\n\nSo, did boredom become fuel for meanness?\n\nParticipants who were strongly inclined to enjoy seeing others suffer were more likely to reduce the other person’s reward after watching the stone video than after watching the magician video. Among those with a weaker tendency, there was no clear difference.\n\nYou might want to dismiss this by saying, “So it really is a matter of personality.” But there’s more to the story.\n\nIn the next experiment, participants were again randomly assigned to groups. But this time, there was no option to increase the reward. The only choice was to reduce it. This time, regardless of how strongly participants were inclined to enjoy seeing others suffer, those who watched the stone video were more likely to reduce another person’s reward than those in the other conditions.\n\nWell, if we focus only on personality, we may miss something. Along with “what kind of person someone is,” there’s also “what they can do in that situation.” We thought we were reading a profile of the person, but now we start noticing the stage setup, too.\n\nBut it’s too soon to jump to “Add a button to increase rewards and bring world peace!” The two were separate experiments. We can’t say that the difference in choices alone caused the difference in results. We don’t know that offering an “increase” option can prevent meanness.\n\nThe experiment also dealt only with brief boredom and actions that reduced another person’s reward. We can’t apply its findings directly to everyday meanness or boredom that lasts a long time.\n\nEven so, our view of boring moments shifts a little. We can ask not only “What mustn’t I do?” but also “What else can I do instead?” Before looking into someone’s personality, look at the menu in front of us. The stone may not move for five minutes, but the question we ask shifts a little.\n\n## In one line\nStone-watchers were more likely to cut another person’s reward—only those high in sadistic tendencies in one experiment, but people at every level in another. The findings suggest that the choices available in a situation may matter alongside personality. But these separate, brief experiments do not show that offering an increase option prevents meanness or that the findings apply to everyday life.\n\n\n【匿名の選定結果】\n\nS1:\n  1. cut someone else's reward\n  2. become fuel for meanness\n  3. bring world peace\n  4. looking into someone’s personality\n  5. a matter of personality\n\nS2:\n  1. too soon to jump to\n  2. fuel for ~\n  3. dismiss something by saying\n  4. were strongly inclined to\n  5. regardless of how strongly\n\nS3:\n  1. sadistic\n  2. turn on\n  3. apply one's findings\n  4. jump to\n  5. stone video\n\nS4:\n  1. all of a sudden\n  2. inclined to enjoy suffering\n  3. dismiss this by saying\n  4. jump to\n  5. look into someone’s personality\n\nS5:\n  1. be randomly assigned to ~\n  2. be strongly inclined to ~\n  3. dismiss something by saying ~\n  4. There’s more to the story\n  5. jump to ~\n\nS6:\n  1. sadistic tendencies\n  2. randomly assigned to groups\n  3. fuel for meanness\n\nS7:\n  1. regardless of how strongly\n  2. too soon to jump to\n  3. apply ~ findings directly to\n  4. choices available in a situation\n  5. matter alongside personality\n",
